U01|X01,C04|HSF.IF.C.7.a|reasoning|Quadratic rules, area and constant second differences over equal intervals; finite data do not prove a unique future rule.|Recognize a quadratic model among familiar families.|For the rule A(s)=s^2, explain why output changes over equal input steps are not constant.|The differences grow; for s=1,2,3 the areas are 1,4,9 and changes are 3 then 5.|Contrast an explicit quadratic rule with merely seeing a curved picture.
U02|P02|HSA.SSE.A.1.a,HSA.REI.B.4|structured_response|Standard form ax^2+bx+c with a nonzero; missing terms have coefficient zero.|Identify all three coefficients including a missing linear term.|Give a,b,c for -2x^2+5=0.|{"a":-2,"b":0,"c":5}|Use a missing term to test coefficient-position assumptions.
U03|U01,N04|HSF.IF.B.4|structured_response|Second differences only for equally spaced inputs; model assumption explicit.|Use first and second differences to support a quadratic model.|At x=0,1,2,3 the outputs are 1,4,9,16. Find the second differences.|[2,2]; consistent with a quadratic model on these inputs.|Change input spacing to test the equal-interval condition.
U04|U01,C02,C09|HSF.IF.C.7.a|structured_response|Parabola orientation and intercepts; real zeros may be absent.|Interpret graph features from a factored quadratic.|For y=(x-1)(x-3), give the x-intercepts and y-intercept.|{"x_intercepts":[[1,0],[3,0]],"y_intercept":[0,3]}|Distinguish roots from intercept points and check x=0 separately.
U05|U04|HSF.IF.B.4,HSF.IF.C.7.a|structured_response|Vertex, symmetry and extrema in a stated real or contextual domain.|Interpret vertex form and the sign of the leading factor.|For y=-(x-2)^2+9, state the vertex, axis and maximum.|{"vertex":[2,9],"axis":"x=2","maximum":9}|Contrast maximum output with the input at which it occurs.
U06|U02,U04,P08|HSF.IF.C.8.a,HSA.SSE.B.3|reasoning|Standard, factored and vertex forms; features extracted without unnecessary full expansion.|Choose the form that reveals a requested feature.|For x^2-4x+3=(x-1)(x-3)=(x-2)^2-1, which form directly shows the minimum?|Vertex form shows minimum -1 at x=2.|Ask which form is useful for zeros versus initial value versus vertex.
U07|X10,Q05|8.EE.A.2,HSA.REI.B.4.b|solution_set|Real solutions of x^2=k; both signs when k>0, one zero root when k=0.|Distinguish the square-root function from solving a squared equation.|Solve x^2=49 over the real numbers.|[-7,7]|Contrast the solution set with the principal value sqrt(49).
U08|F09,Q02|HSA.REI.B.4.b|solution_set|Integer-coefficient quadratics with accessible integer factorizations; solve over the reals and rearrange to zero first. Fractional coefficients require the declared clearing step.|Factor, solve every factor and verify the original equation.|Solve x^2-x-6=0.|[-2,3]|Separate a factoring probe from a zero-product probe.
U09|U07,Q06|HSA.REI.B.4.b|solution_set|Quadratics in square form; sign and exact roots preserved.|Isolate the square and solve both branches.|Solve (x-2)^2=9.|[-1,5]|Check whether the square-root step produces both signs before undoing the shift.
U10|P08,Q06,N09|HSA.REI.B.4.a,HSA.SSE.B.3.b|equation|Monic completing the square, including odd linear coefficients.|Add the required square while preserving equality.|Rewrite x^2+6x+2=0 as (x+h)^2=k.|(x+3)^2=7|Probe half the linear coefficient before squaring it.
U11|U10,Q10|HSA.REI.B.4.a|equation|Nonmonic quadratics; normalize the leading coefficient or preserve an equivalent outside factor.|Complete the square without losing constant scaling.|Rewrite 2x^2+8x+1=0 in square form.|(x+2)^2=7/2|Distinguish dividing all terms by a from halving the original b.
U12|U02,X10,Q10|HSA.REI.B.4.b|solution_set|Quadratic formula with a nonzero; exact real roots and declared approximations.|Substitute signed coefficients with correct grouping and state both roots.|Solve x^2-2x-1=0 exactly.|["1-sqrt(2)","1+sqrt(2)"]|Probe coefficient extraction separately from the minus sign in -b.
U13|U12|HSA.REI.B.4.b|classification|Discriminant classifies the number of real roots; complex construction remains H07.|Interpret a negative discriminant over the real domain.|How many real solutions does x^2+2x+5=0 have?|None; the discriminant is -16.|Contrast no real roots with no roots in every possible number system.
U14|U08,U04|HSA.REI.D.10,HSF.IF.C.8.a|structured_response|Real equation roots correspond to graph points with y=0.|Connect a solution set to x-intercepts.|For y=x^2-5x+6, connect the equation's roots to graph intercepts.|Roots 2 and 3 correspond to points (2,0) and (3,0).|Distinguish x-coordinates from full ordered pairs.
U15|U08,U09,U12,M05|HSA.REI.B.4,HSF.IF.B.4|reasoning|Strategic choice among known methods; contextual feasibility and exactness included.|Choose and justify an efficient valid solving method.|Choose an efficient method for (x-4)^2=7 and solve exactly.|Use square roots; x=4-sqrt(7) or x=4+sqrt(7).|Contrast choosing a valid method with choosing an unnecessarily long one.
U16|U11,E11,Q13|HSA.REI.B.4.a|reasoning|Derivation for ax^2+bx+c=0 with a nonzero; real-root condition stated.|Derive the formula through equivalent transformations and completing the square.|Derive the quadratic formula from ax^2+bx+c=0 for a nonzero.|Divide by a, complete the square, take both square-root branches, then isolate x to obtain (-b +/- sqrt(b^2-4ac))/(2a); real roots require b^2-4ac>=0.|Check equality preservation and the denominator 2a rather than accepting a memorized formula.
G01|L01,N14|HSF.LE.A.1.a,HSF.LE.A.1.c|classification|Equal input intervals; fixed differences versus fixed positive ratios.|Distinguish additive and multiplicative patterns under the stated model family.|A quantity doubles each day. Is the change additive or multiplicative?|Multiplicative; the ratio for each day is 2.|Contrast constant percentage with constant amount.
G02|G01,X01,A10|HSF.LE.B.5,HSF.LE.A.2|reasoning|y=a*b^x with b>0 and b!=1; nonnegative quantities used for entry contexts.|Interpret initial value and growth factor.|For P(t)=50*1.2^t, explain 50 and 1.2.|Initial quantity 50; each unit of t multiplies the quantity by 1.2.|Distinguish a multiplier from the amount added.
G03|G02,A09|HSF.IF.A.2|scalar|Evaluate familiar exponential functions; negative inputs conditional on X08.|Compute the modeled value at a specified input.|For P(t)=50*2^t, find P(3).|400|Separate exponent evaluation from multiplication by the initial value.
G04|G03,C02|HSF.IF.C.7.e|graph_spec|Positive-base exponential graphs; intercept and end behavior; logarithmic and trigonometric families excluded from core scope.|Graph growth or decay with meaningful scale and domain.|Sketch y=2^x with three exact points and its horizontal limiting value.|Points (-1,1/2),(0,1),(1,2); approaches y=0 as x decreases without bound and never reaches 0.|Contrast a limiting value with an attained intercept.
G05|G02,N12|HSF.LE.A.1.c,HSF.LE.B.5|expression|Percent increase and decrease multipliers; valid nonnegative percentage contexts.|Translate a repeated percentage change to a factor.|A value decreases by 15% each year. Write its annual multiplier.|0.85|Contrast the percent lost with the fraction retained.
G06|G05,C04|HSF.LE.A.2,HSF.BF.A.1.a|model|Construct a*b^t from context or suitable data; time origin explicit.|Build and interpret an exponential model.|A culture starts at 80 units and triples each hour. Write P(t), with t in hours.|P(t)=80*3^t|Ask for the value at t=0 to test initial-value placement.
G07|L05,G04|HSF.LE.A.1,HSF.IF.C.9|reasoning|Compare linear and exponential models over stated intervals and contexts.|Distinguish first-step similarity from different later growth.|Compare A(n)=10+3n and B(n)=10*1.3^n at n=1 and n=2.|Both give 13 at n=1; at n=2, A=16 and B=16.9.|Use a second interval to distinguish additive growth from percent growth.
G08|G03,X05|HSF.LE.A.4|scalar|Optional common-base exponential equations solvable by exponent reasoning; logarithmic methods not required here.|Rewrite both sides to a common base.|Solve 2^(x+1)=16.|3|Contrast an unknown exponent with a coefficient multiplying a power.
G09|L01,C06|HSF.IF.A.3,HSF.BF.A.2|structured_response|Arithmetic sequences as functions on positive integers; indexing origin explicit.|Write equivalent recursive and explicit rules.|An arithmetic sequence has a_1=7 and common difference 4. Give both rules.|a_n=7+4(n-1); a_1=7 and a_n=a_(n-1)+4 for n>=2.|Probe n=1 to distinguish n from n-1 indexing.
G10|G02,C06|HSF.IF.A.3,HSF.BF.A.2|structured_response|Geometric sequences with stated first term and ratio; indexing explicit.|Translate between repeated multiplication and an explicit exponent.|A geometric sequence has a_1=3 and ratio 2. Give recursive and explicit rules.|a_n=3*2^(n-1); a_1=3 and a_n=2*a_(n-1) for n>=2.|Compare a_n-a_(n-1) with a_n/a_(n-1).
G11|G07,U01|HSF.LE.A.3|reasoning|Eventual growth of specified exponential and polynomial models; finite samples are not a universal proof.|Use tables or graphs to compare growth while stating the inference boundary.|At n=10 compare 2^n with n^2. Does that single comparison prove every later comparison?|1024 exceeds 100; one comparison alone does not prove a claim about all later n.|Distinguish observed dominance from a justified eventual-growth statement.
G12|G02,X05|HSA.SSE.B.3.c,HSF.IF.C.8.b|expression|Core: integer changes of time interval; rational-exponent subperiod variants require H06 and are optional in this profile.|Rewrite an exponential expression to reveal a rate for another time unit.|Rewrite 2^(3t) as b^t and interpret b.|8^t; the multiplier for each unit of t is 8.|Contrast a change of exponent scale with adding to the base.
R01|X10,N08|HSN.RN.B.3|classification|Rational means a quotient of integers with nonzero denominator; irrational is a real number not of that form.|Classify examples using structure rather than decimal length alone.|Classify sqrt(9), sqrt(2), and 0.333... with repeating 3s.|sqrt(9) and repeating 0.333... are rational; sqrt(2) is irrational.|Contrast a terminating approximation with the exact irrational value.
R02|X10,X03|HSA.REI.B.4.b,8.EE.A.2|expression|Simplify real square roots by extracting square factors; principal root and nonnegative radicand preserved.|Express an exact root in simplest radical form.|Simplify sqrt(72).|6*sqrt(2)|Contrast sqrt(a+b) with valid factor-based simplification.
R03|R02,E03|HSN.RN.A.2|expression|Optional operations with real radicals; nonnegative radicands and like-radical collection.|Combine equivalent radical units correctly.|Simplify sqrt(8)+sqrt(18).|5*sqrt(2)|Probe simplification before testing collection of like radicals.
R04|N06,A05|HSA.REI.A.2|reasoning|Expressions with zero denominators are undefined; restrictions belong to the original expression.|Identify an excluded input before simplifying.|For 1/(x-3), which real input is excluded and why?|x=3, because the denominator would be zero.|Contrast a zero numerator with a zero denominator.
R05|R04,F01|HSA.APR.D.7|expression|Optional rational expressions; simplify factors while preserving original exclusions.|Cancel common nonzero factors without canceling terms.|Simplify (x^2-9)/(x-3), stating restrictions.|x+3, with x!=3.|Contrast canceling a common factor with canceling one term of a sum.
R06|R05,Q11|HSA.REI.A.2|solution_set|Optional rational equations; original denominator restrictions and checking required.|Solve and reject excluded or extraneous candidates.|Solve x/(x-1)=2 over the real numbers.|[2], with original restriction x!=1.|Use a second item whose only algebraic candidate is excluded.
R07|X10,Q09,Q02|HSA.REI.A.2|solution_set|Optional radical equations; squaring may introduce extraneous candidates.|Check every candidate in the original radical equation.|Solve sqrt(x+1)=x-1 over the real numbers.|[3]|Compare the squared equation's candidates 0 and 3 in the original equation.
R08|R01,N09,N10|HSN.RN.B.3|reasoning|Closure arguments and counterexamples; a nonzero rational multiplier is required for the product claim.|Explain valid rational-irrational conclusions without overgeneralizing.|Classify 3+sqrt(2) and sqrt(2)+(-sqrt(2)), with reasons.|The first is irrational; the second is 0 and rational. Two irrational summands need not have an irrational sum.|Vary the number types and test whether the proposed rule is universal.
D01||HSS.ID.B.5,HSS.ID.A.1|classification|Categorical versus quantitative data; statistical questions anticipate variation.|Identify data type and a question requiring a distribution.|Is favorite sport categorical or quantitative? Does asking a class for their favorite sports anticipate variation?|Categorical; yes, different students may name different sports.|Contrast numerical category labels with measured quantities.
D02|D01,N20|HSS.ID.A.1|graph_spec|Dot plots, histograms and box plots; scale, labels and bin conventions explicit.|Construct and interpret an appropriate one-variable display.|For data 1,1,2,4, describe a dot plot with a unit scale.|Two dots above 1, one above 2, one above 4; labeled numerical axis.|Separate frequency height from the numerical variable's values.
D03|N17,N18|HSS.ID.A.2|structured_response|Mean and median with correct ordering and sample size.|Compute both centers and explain their meanings.|Find the mean and median of 2,2,3,9.|{"mean":4,"median":"5/2"}|Contrast ordering for the median with totaling for the mean.
D04|D03,N13|HSS.ID.A.2|structured_response|Quartiles and IQR; the declared median-of-halves convention excludes the median for odd n.|Compute quartiles using a stated convention.|For 1,2,3,4,5,6,7, exclude the median from both halves. Find Q1,Q3 and IQR.|{"Q1":2,"Q3":6,"IQR":4}|Distinguish quartile convention differences from arithmetic errors.
D05|D03,N15|HSS.ID.A.2|reasoning|Population standard deviation interpreted with units; technology may compute it; normal-curve inference excluded.|Compare variability around the mean and interpret an SD value.|Which set has larger population SD: A={4,4,4} or B={2,4,6}? Explain.|B; both means are 4, but A has no variation and B has nonzero deviations.|Contrast spread with a different mean or a different number of observations.
D06|D02,D03,D04,D05|HSS.ID.A.3|structured_response|Distribution shape, center, spread and contextual treatment of outliers.|Compare summaries before and after an extreme observation.|For data 2,3,4, find mean and median before and after adding 30.|Before: mean=3, median=3. After: mean=39/4, median=7/2.|Check the observed effect instead of assuming that every unusual value should be deleted.
D07|C02,D01|HSS.ID.B.6|reasoning|Scatterplots, direction, form and unusual points; association alone is not causation.|Describe an observed association without a causal claim.|Describe the association for points (1,2),(2,4),(3,6).|Perfect positive linear association in these three observations; no causal conclusion follows.|Separate direction and form from a causal explanation.
D08|D07,L13|HSS.ID.B.6.a,HSS.ID.B.6.c,HSS.ID.C.7|equation|Approximate linear fit with technology when appropriate; contextual slope and interpolation limits.|Construct a fitted line and interpret its coefficients.|Fit a line to (0,1),(1,3),(2,5).|y_hat=2*x+1|Compare fitting a trend with joining every observed point.
D09|D08,D05|HSS.ID.B.6.b,HSS.ID.C.8|structured_response|Residual=observed-predicted; correlation describes linear association and is not prediction accuracy.|Compute a residual and interpret correlation without a causal or percent-correct claim.|A line predicts y=2x+1. At x=3 the observed y is 8. Find the residual; explain what a positive r describes.|Residual 1; positive r describes positive linear association, not the fraction of correct predictions.|Contrast residual sign with a point being above or below the fitted line.
D10|D07|HSS.ID.C.9|reasoning|Observational association, possible confounding and causal limitations.|Reject an unsupported causal conclusion and name a plausible alternative.|Ice-cream sales and swimming incidents rise together. Does that prove ice cream causes incidents?|No; a factor such as hotter weather may affect both.|Ask what design or information would distinguish competing causal explanations.
D11|D01,N14|HSS.ID.B.5|structured_response|Joint, marginal and conditional relative frequencies; denominator follows the reference group.|Compute and distinguish three reference groups.|Of 20 swimmers, 12 cycle. Of 30 non-swimmers, 8 cycle. Give the cycling fraction among swimmers, swimmer-and-cyclist fraction overall, and swimmer fraction overall.|{"conditional":"3/5","joint":"6/25","marginal":"2/5"}|Hold the numerator fixed and change the stated reference group.
M01|A10,N20|HSN.Q.A.1,HSN.Q.A.2|model|Identify quantities, define symbols and maintain units across representations.|Choose quantities that make a contextual relationship meaningful.|A taxi charges 5 dollars plus 2 dollars per km. Define variables and write the cost model.|d is distance in km, C is dollars; C=5+2d.|Contrast dollars with dollars per kilometer.
M02|A08,A10,Q01|HSA.CED.A.1,HSA.CED.A.2,HSF.BF.A.1.a|model|Translate situations into equations or functions; representation choice justified.|Construct a model with defined quantities rather than guessing an operation.|A tank begins with 12 liters and drains 2 liters per minute. Model its volume before emptying.|V=12-2t, with t in minutes and V in liters; 0<=t<=6.|Distinguish initial amount, direction of change and physical domain.
M03|M02,L14,G01,U01|HSF.LE.A.1,HSF.LE.A.2,HSF.IF.B.4|reasoning|Choose among specified linear, quadratic and exponential families; state assumptions and finite-data limits.|Use differences, ratios or contextual structure to justify a family choice.|Under a choice of linear, quadratic or exponential models, which fits outputs 2,6,18,54 at equal input steps?|An exponential model fits, with common factor 3; the finite data alone do not establish a unique unrestricted rule.|Compare a model-family choice with a claim about every possible function.
M04|C08,A10,I01|HSF.IF.B.5,HSA.CED.A.3|interval|Realistic continuous, discrete and nonnegative domains; restrictions from the situation retained.|Restrict a model to feasible inputs.|A model counts the cost of n tickets. Can n=-1 or n=2.5 be used as a ticket count?|Neither; n must be a nonnegative integer under this situation.|Contrast algebraically evaluable inputs with physically meaningful inputs.
M05|Q02,A10|HSA.CED.A.3,HSA.REI.A.1|reasoning|Check mathematical validity and contextual feasibility separately.|Reject a mathematical candidate that violates the original situation.|A model for a length yields candidates -3 cm and 5 cm. Which is feasible as the length?|5 cm; a negative length is infeasible in this model.|Distinguish a negative quantity that is valid in another context from a negative physical length.
M06|C10,M01|HSF.IF.C.9,HSA.CED.A.2|model|Consistency of units, scales and the same relationship across representations.|Translate and reconcile a rule, table, graph and context.|For C=5+2d, give table values at d=0,1,2 and describe the graph.|Costs 5,7,9; line through (0,5) with slope 2 dollars per kilometer on the stated domain.|Probe whether a changed scale or unit is mistaken for a changed relationship.
M07|Q03,E11|HSA.REI.A.1,HSA.REI.C.5|reasoning|Justification of transformations; a sequence of written steps alone is not an explanation.|Explain why a solution method preserves the intended solution set.|Explain why adding x-y=1 to x+y=7 gives a valid consequence 2x=8.|Equal quantities may be added on each side; corresponding sides sum to the new equality.|Contrast a valid consequence with claiming that one new equation alone replaces the entire system.
M08|M02,M05|HSN.Q.A.2,HSF.IF.B.4|reasoning|Assumptions, model limits, interpolation and extrapolation; no unsupported causal claim.|State a limitation that matters to a model's use.|A linear plant-growth model is fitted from days 1-10. What is a limitation of predicting day 500?|The observed growth rate may not persist; day 500 is far outside the fitted range.|Distinguish mathematical evaluation from evidence that the model remains appropriate.
M09|M01,N20|HSN.Q.A.3|quantity|Accuracy appropriate to stated measurement limits; no universal significant-figure rule invented.|Report a modeled quantity with defensible precision.|A distance estimate is 12.347 km, but the source supports only tenths of a kilometer. Report it appropriately.|{"value":"12.3","unit":"km"}|Contrast exact algebraic value with a justified reported measurement.
H01|C13,I06|HSA.REI.B.3|solution_set|Optional absolute-value equations and inequalities; multiple branches and empty sets included.|Interpret distance and retain every valid branch.|Solve abs(2x-3)=5.|[-1,4]|Contrast a two-branch equation with taking only the positive internal expression.
H02|C11,Q09|HSF.IF.C.7.b,HSA.REI.B.3|solution_set|Optional piecewise equations; candidates must satisfy their branch conditions.|Solve each relevant branch and filter candidates.|Let f(x)=x+1 for x<2 and 2x for x>=2. Solve f(x)=4.|[2]|Check the branch condition after solving the branch equation.
H03|C16,C19,Q13|HSF.BF.A.1.c,HSF.BF.B.4|structured_response|Optional composition and broader inverse functions; domain restrictions required.|Compose in the stated order and reverse a one-to-one rule.|For f(x)=2x+1 and g(x)=x^2, find f(g(x)) and the inverse of f.|{"composition":"2*x^2+1","inverse":"(x-1)/2"}|Contrast f(g(x)), g(f(x)) and 1/f(x).
H04|C15,C17|HSF.BF.B.3|reasoning|Optional broader transformations, input scaling and symmetry across loaded families; no trigonometry scope implied.|Explain combined input and output transformations.|For f(x)=x^2, describe g(x)=-2f(3(x-1))+4.|Horizontal compression by 1/3, shift right 1, reflection in x-axis, vertical stretch by 2, shift up 4; g=-18(x-1)^2+4.|Isolate one transformation to distinguish horizontal scaling from vertical scaling.
H05|P07,Q06|HSA.APR.D.6,HSA.APR.B.2|structured_response|Optional polynomial division and remainder; rational-function domain kept separate from polynomial identity.|Find quotient and remainder and state any original restriction.|Divide x^3-1 by x-1. If viewed as a quotient function, state its restriction.|Quotient x^2+x+1, remainder 0; quotient-function domain excludes x=1.|Contrast a polynomial identity with the domain of the original rational function.
H06|X05,X10,X11|HSN.RN.A.1,HSN.RN.A.2|scalar|Optional rational exponents; positive bases by default and explicit real-root conventions.|Connect fractional powers with roots and powers.|Evaluate 27^(2/3) over the positive real domain.|9|Contrast the denominator's root operation with multiplying by a fraction.
H07|U12,R02|HSA.REI.B.4.b|solution_set|Optional complex roots with i^2=-1; distinguish no real solution from complex solutions.|Construct and verify nonreal quadratic roots.|Solve x^2+4=0 over the complex numbers.|["-2i","2i"]|Check the stated number system before deciding that there is no solution.
H08|G03,X08|HSF.LE.A.4,HSF.BF.B.5|scalar|Optional logarithms as inverse exponent questions; positive argument and valid base.|Translate between exponential and logarithmic form.|Evaluate log base 2 of 8.|3|Contrast the logarithm with division by the base.
H09|R05,C08,L11|HSF.IF.C.7.d|structured_response|Optional rational functions; holes distinguished from asymptotes and excluded inputs.|Analyze basic domain and asymptotes.|For f(x)=(x+1)/(x-2), give the excluded input and vertical and horizontal asymptotes.|{"excluded":2,"vertical":"x=2","horizontal":"y=1"}|Use a removable-factor example to distinguish a hole from a vertical asymptote.
H10|S09,U12|HSA.REI.C.7|point_set|Optional broader elementary nonlinear systems, including two quadratics; no universal solving claim.|Substitute or compare relations and verify every intersection.|Solve y=x^2 and y=2x^2-1 over the real numbers.|[[-1,1],[1,1]]|Check that both coordinates satisfy both original relations.
